<translate>

== Overview ==

This page is a cheat heet for the OpenCASCADE Draw tool.  Important links:
* [[#Cheat_sheet|The cheat sheet]]
* The [https://dev.opencascade.org/doc/overview/html/occt_user_guides__test_harness.html OpenCASCADE Draw user guide]
* [[#Background_information|Background information]]

=== Cheat sheet ===

==== Initialization ====

Load all modules:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
pload ALL
}}
<translate>

Load a Draw/tcl file (for example {{Incode|test.tcl}}):

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
source test.tcl
}}
<translate>

Find files in your system:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
pwd
cd ..
ls
}}
<translate>

==== Restore a BREP file ====

Restore file {{Incode|model.test}} into variable {{Incode|model}}:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
restore model.test model
}}
<translate>

==== Display a model ====

Display model {{Incode|model}}:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
vdisplay model
vfit
}}
<translate>

==== Find an edge or face in the model ====

Display the model first.

For edges, choose selection mode 2, for faces selection mode 4:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
vselmod 2 on
}}
<translate>

Pick an edge or face in the window and find the number of the edge or face:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
vstate
}}
<translate>

It will say something like this:

</translate>
{{Code|code=
Locally selected sub-shapes within model:
       Edge #4
}}
<translate>

Explode model {{Incode|model}} with {{Incode|e}} for edges or {{Incode|f}} for faces:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
explode model e
}}
<translate>

You can access edge #4 with {{Incode|model_4}}, for example displaying it:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
vdisplay model_4
}}
<translate>

Get a subshape of a model {{Incode|model}} directly, face 3 and display it:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
subshape model f 3
vdisplay model_3
}}
<translate>

==== Checking shapes ====

Various commands to check shape {{Incode|shape}}:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
checkshape shape
bopcheck shape
whatis shape
nbshapes shape
}}
<translate>

==== Tolerances ====

Investigate the tolerances of shape {{Incode|shape}}:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
toler shape
dump shape
}}
<translate>

Set the tolerance of a shape {{Incode|shape}}:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
dset tolerance 0.0001
settolerance shape tolerance
}}
<translate>

==== Fillets and chamfers ====

Create a fillet with radius 0.5 or a chamfer with distance 0.5 on the first edge of {{Incode|model}} and store it in shape {{Incode|result}}.  Notice that the position of the radius and distance is reversed for the two different calls, for fillet:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
blend result model 0.5 model_1
}}
<translate>

For chamfer:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
chamf result model model_1 0.5
}}
<translate>

==== Create a box ====

Create a box {{Incode|b}}

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
box b 0 0 0 1 2 3
}}
<translate>

=== Background information ===

==== Installation ====

The Draw program is typically installed together with your OpenCASCADE distribution.  On Linux, you can typically find the program in your path under {{Incode|DRAWEXE}}.

If you followed [[Compile with OCCT]], you can find the Draw tool of the custom version in your distribution directory, in the {{Incode|bin}} directory of your distribution.

To make sure that I load an OCCT Draw from a particular distribution, I have the following function in my {{Incode|$HOME/.bashrc}}:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=tcl|code=
function set-occt() {
    if [ -z "$1" ]; then
	echo "Usage: set-occt <dir>"
	return 1
    fi
    if [ ! -d "$1" ]; then
	echo "Error: Directory $1 does not exist"
	return 1
    fi
    pushd "$1" > /dev/null
    export CASROOT=
    export CSF_OCCTResourcePath=$PWD/share/opencascade/resources
    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$PWD/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
    export PATH=$PWD/bin:$PATH
    source ./bin/env.sh
    popd > /dev/null
}
}}
<translate>

Suppose I want to make use of the OCCT version that is in the following installation directory {{Incode|/scratch/dist-occt-main}}, I execute the following in my bash shell:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=bash|code=
set-occt /scratch/dist-occt-main
}}
<translate>

I verify whether the correct Draw program is really in my path:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=bash|code=
which DRAWEXE
}}
<translate>

This should print:

</translate>
{{Code||code=
/scratch/dist-occt-main/bin/DRAWEXE
}}
<translate>

You can verify the version number with:

</translate>
{{Code|lang=bash|code=
DRAWEXE --help
}}
<translate>


</translate>
